---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const image = (name: string) => `${base}/images/excel/${name}.png`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Build a transposition tool in Excel">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 01 / EXCEL</p>
    <h1>
      Build a transposition
      <br />
      tool in Excel<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>From your first formula to movable columns and a complete decoded message.</p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>START FROM A BLANK WORKBOOK</span>
      <span>MICROSOFT 365 / EXCEL 2024</span>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
      <a href="#basics">01 / Cells and formulas</a>
      <a href="#references">02 / References and $</a>
      <a href="#inputs">03 / Prepare the text</a>
      <a href="#letters">04 / One letter per cell</a>
      <a href="#grid">05 / Make the grid</a>
      <a href="#move">06 / Move columns</a>
      <a href="#output">07 / Copy the answer</a>
      <a href="#refresh">08 / Try another message</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <p>
        Transposition changes the order of letters. We will build a sheet that splits ciphertext
        into rows of a chosen length, then move the same columns in every row. Our small example
        comes from{" "}
        <a href="https://www.cipherchallenge.org/challenge/challenge-3/">
          National Cipher Challenge, Challenge 3 Part B
        </a>
        :
      </p>
      <pre>TEZEM HNETI EIRND</pre>
      <p>
        With five columns, putting them in order <strong>5, 2, 4, 1, 3</strong> reveals{" "}
        <code>MEETZ INTHE DINER</code>. Read that as “MEET Z IN THE DINER”. Five-letter groups are
        display spacing, not word boundaries.
      </p>
      <h2 id="basics">1. A cell can hold an instruction</h2>
      <p>
        Open a blank workbook. Each square is a <strong>cell</strong>. The letters along the top
        name columns; the numbers down the side name rows. <strong>B3</strong> means column B, row
        3. Click a cell, type, then press Enter. To replace its contents, select it and type again.
        Double-click it or press F2 to edit what is already there.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>formula</strong> starts with <code>=</code>. Type <code>=1+2</code> into B3 and
        press Enter. The cell displays <strong>3</strong>; selecting it shows the instruction in the
        formula bar above the grid. Ordinary text does not need an equals sign.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rename the first sheet <strong>Basics</strong> by double-clicking its tab at the bottom. Use
        the + beside the tabs to add three more sheets called <strong>Inputs</strong>,{" "}
        <strong>Arrange</strong> and <strong>Readout</strong>. These names matter because formulas
        will refer to them.
      </p>
      <h2 id="references">2. Refer to another cell, then fix a reference</h2>
      <p>
        On Basics, enter <strong>10</strong> in A3 and <strong>20</strong> in A4. Replace B3 with:
      </p>
      <pre>=A3+1</pre>
      <p>
        B3 now shows 11. Change A3 and B3 updates automatically: the formula reads another cell's
        value. In C3 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=$A$3+1</pre>
      <p>
        Both cells show the same answer. Now copy B3:C3 (select them and press Ctrl+C), select B4,
        and paste with Ctrl+V. B4 becomes <code>=A4+1</code>, so it shows 21. C4 keeps{" "}
        <code>=$A$3+1</code>, so it still shows 11.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>A3</code> is relative: copying down changes the row; copying across changes the
          column.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>$A$3</code> fixes both column A and row 3 when copied.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>$A3</code> fixes only the column; <code>A$3</code> fixes only the row.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("references")}
          alt="Actual Excel cells: copying down changes A3 to A4 while dollar A dollar 3 stays fixed; answers are 11, 21 and 11."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Basics!A1:F5. The two columns on the right display the formulas so you can compare them.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        When editing a reference in a formula, F4 cycles through these dollar-sign choices. Dollar
        signs control copying; they do not turn a formula into a fixed letter. We will fix the
        letters as values later.
      </p>
      <h2 id="inputs">3. Build the text preparation one step at a time</h2>
      <p>
        On Inputs, put the labels from this table in column A. Enter each value or formula in the
        matching B cell. Functions are named instructions: the inputs go inside parentheses,
        separated by commas. Text inside a formula needs double quotation marks. These examples use
        English function names; some Excel settings require semicolons instead of commas.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Label in column A</th>
            <th>Cell</th>
            <th>Enter</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Key length</td>
            <td>B2</td>
            <td>
              <code>5</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ciphertext</td>
            <td>B3</td>
            <td>
              <code>TEZEM HNETI EIRND</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Remove spaces</td>
            <td>B4</td>
            <td>
              <code>=SUBSTITUTE(B3," ","")</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Uppercase</td>
            <td>B5</td>
            <td>
              <code>=UPPER(B4)</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Letter count</td>
            <td>B6</td>
            <td>
              <code>=LEN(B5)</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grid rows</td>
            <td>B7</td>
            <td>
              <code>=ROUNDUP(B6/B2,0)</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <code>SUBSTITUTE</code> takes the text in B3, finds a space (<code>" "</code>), and replaces
        it with nothing (<code>""</code>). <code>UPPER</code> reads that result from B4 and
        capitalises it. <code>LEN</code> reads B5 and counts its characters. This is a{" "}
        <strong>chain</strong>: one formula's output becomes the next formula's input.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally B7 divides the letter count by the key length. <code>ROUNDUP(...,0)</code> rounds up
        to a whole number of rows: 15 ÷ 5 needs three rows; 16 ÷ 5 needs four.
      </p>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("inputs")}
          alt="Actual Excel Inputs sheet with key length 5, spaced ciphertext, cleaned text, 15 letters and 3 grid rows."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Inputs!A1:B7. Check these results before continuing.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>For now, paste one line containing letters and ordinary spaces only.</strong> This
          simple cleanup leaves punctuation, tabs and line breaks intact. For a longer challenge,
          use the site's transposition tool with “No spacing” and punctuation off, then copy its
          input letters. Do not put zero or a negative number in the key-length cell.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2 id="letters">4. Extract one letter, then make a list</h2>
      <p>Try this in an empty cell on Basics:</p>
      <pre>=MID("HELLO",2,1)</pre>
      <p>
        The answer is E. <code>MID</code> takes some text, a starting position, and how many
        characters to take. Positions start at 1. Try changing 2 to 4, or the final 1 to 2.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Inputs, type <strong>Position</strong> in D1 and <strong>Letter</strong> in E1. In D2
        enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=SEQUENCE(B6)</pre>
      <p>
        This produces the numbers 1 to 15 down the column. One formula has produced several cells:
        Excel calls this a <strong>spill</strong>. Keep the cells below D2 empty. Click the top cell
        to edit the formula; the other results belong to it.
      </p>
      <p>First try this in E2:</p>
      <pre>=MID($B$5,D2,1)</pre>
      <p>
        It takes one letter from the cleaned text at the position given by D2. B5 stays fixed if we
        copy the formula. We could copy E2 down, but there is a shorter way. Replace it with:
      </p>
      <pre>=MID($B$5,D2#,1)</pre>
      <p>
        The <code>#</code> means <strong>all of the spilled results starting at D2</strong>. Instead
        of reading just position 1, MID reads the complete list of positions and spills the letters
        down E. If Excel shows <code>#SPILL!</code>, clear anything blocking the result area.
      </p>
      <h2 id="grid">5. Wrap the letters into rows</h2>
      <p>Leave F empty as a gap. In G1 enter:</p>
      <pre>=SEQUENCE(1,B2)</pre>
      <p>
        Here SEQUENCE makes <strong>one row</strong> and B2 columns. With a key length of 5, the
        headings 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 appear across G1:K1. In G2 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=WRAPROWS(E2#,B2,"")</pre>
      <p>
        <code>WRAPROWS</code> takes the letter list E2#, places B2 letters in each row, and uses
        empty text for any unused cells in a short final row. The number of columns follows the key
        length automatically.
      </p>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("grid")}
          alt="Actual Excel generated grid with original headers 1 to 5 and rows T E Z E M, H N E T I, E I R N D."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Inputs!D1:K17. D and E show the intermediate lists; G:K show the five-column grid.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        If you see <code>#NAME?</code>, check the spelling and your Excel version. WRAPROWS and
        TOCOL require Microsoft 365 or Excel 2024. Do not type into individual spilled cells.
      </p>
      <h2 id="move">6. Make the letters movable</h2>
      <p>
        Select Inputs!G1:K4: the five headers and all three data rows. Copy, switch to Arrange,
        select A1, then choose <strong>Home → Paste ▼ → Values</strong> (the 123 icon). The letters
        are now ordinary values, independent of their original formulas.
      </p>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("before")}
          alt="Actual Excel Arrange sheet before reordering: headers 1 2 3 4 5 above the fixed ciphertext letters."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Arrange!A1:E4 after Paste Values. Selecting a letter should show that letter, not a
          formula.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Paste Values is the important step.</strong> Freeze Panes only keeps headings
          visible while scrolling; it does not freeze calculations. Leave the generated grid on
          Inputs intact so you can make a fresh copy.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        On Arrange, select the whole <strong>E column</strong> by clicking its letter at the top.
        Point to the selection border until the move pointer appears. Hold <strong>Shift</strong>,
        drag to just before column A, and release when you see the insertion line. The original
        header 5 should now be first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next move original header <strong>2</strong> (now in column C) to just before column B. Then
        move original header <strong>4</strong> (now in column E) to just before column C. Your
        headers should read <strong>5, 2, 4, 1, 3</strong>. Always follow the numbered headers,
        because Excel's column letters describe positions and stay A, B, C, D, E.
      </p>
      <p>
        If dragging is awkward, select the source column, press Ctrl+X, right-click the destination
        column letter, and choose <strong>Insert Cut Cells</strong>. Ordinary dragging or ordinary
        pasting can overwrite letters, so check the numbered headings after each move.
      </p>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("arranged")}
          alt="Actual Excel reordered columns 5 2 4 1 3 showing MEETZ, INTHE and DINER across the three rows."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The same fixed columns after native Excel insertion moves. Read each row left to right.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 id="output">7. Join the visible grid into one answer</h2>
      <p>
        For a first small experiment on Basics, type <code>MEET</code> in A7 and <code>Z</code> in
        B7. In C7 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A7:B7)</pre>
      <p>
        This joins the cells into MEETZ. The first input is the separator (empty text here); TRUE
        tells Excel to ignore empty cells. <code>A7:B7</code> means the rectangular range from A7
        through B7.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our complete grid needs a little more care. Excel normally changes references to follow
        cells when you move columns. We want to read the <strong>current positions</strong>, A to E,
        in the order visible on Arrange. We will build an address as text, then ask Excel to read
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Readout, put <strong>Grid address</strong> in A2 and enter this small test in B2:
      </p>
      <pre>=ADDRESS(4,5,4)</pre>
      <p>
        The result is <code>E4</code>: row 4, column 5, with relative-style notation (the final 4
        means no dollar signs). Our last row is the data-row count plus one header row. Our last
        column is the key length. Replace B2 with:
      </p>
      <pre>="Arrange!A2:"&amp;ADDRESS(Inputs!B7+1,Inputs!B2,4)</pre>
      <p>
        <code>Inputs!B7</code> reads a cell on another sheet. <code>&amp;</code> joins text
        fragments. The result is the text <code>Arrange!A2:E4</code>. It describes the data
        rectangle without the headers. Because it is text, column moves do not rewrite it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put <strong>Read by rows</strong> in A3. In B3 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=TOCOL(INDIRECT(B2),1,FALSE)</pre>
      <p>
        <code>INDIRECT</code> reads the cells named by the text address in B2. <code>TOCOL</code>{" "}
        turns that rectangle into one column: 1 ignores blank cells, and FALSE scans{" "}
        <strong>across each row</strong> before moving to the next. You should see M, E, E, T, Z, I,
        N, T, H, E, D, I, N, E, R spilling down B.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put <strong>Copy the value in D3</strong> in D2. In D3 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,B3#)</pre>
      <p>
        This joins the entire spilled list. D3 should display <strong>MEETZINTHEDINER</strong>.
        Select D3 and copy it. To paste the answer into another spreadsheet, use{" "}
        <strong>Paste Values</strong>; into a text editor or cipher tool, paste normally. Copy D3
        alone, not the intermediate list.
      </p>
      <figure class="excel-figure">
        <img
          src={image("readout")}
          alt="Actual Excel Readout sheet with address Arrange A2 to E4, the letters listed in row order and joined answer MEETZINTHEDINER."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Readout!A1:D18. This answer was checked after moving the actual Excel columns, not just
          rewriting a key.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 id="refresh">8. Try a different length or ciphertext</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Change Inputs!B2 to a positive whole-number key length and B3 to your new ciphertext. The
          lists and source grid update automatically.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check B7 for the data-row count. Include one extra header row when selecting the source
          grid, and include exactly B2 columns.
        </li>
        <li>
          On Arrange, clear the previous grid before pasting the new source grid as{" "}
          <strong>Values</strong> into A1. This removes stale letters when the new message is
          shorter.
        </li>
        <li>
          Move the numbered columns into a new order. Readout follows the new key length, row count
          and visible order.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check that the output has the same number of letters as Inputs!B6. No letters should be
          added or lost.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Try <code>ABC DEF GH</code> with key length 3: the final row is short, and the blank cell
        should be skipped in the joined output. The sheet treats each row as an independent block. A
        cipher written down columns needs a different reconstruction; this guide builds the block
        method used in Challenge 3 B.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also test a known numeric order without dragging: on a separate sheet, type the
        original column numbers in row 1. For example A1:E1 = 5, 2, 4, 1, 3. In A2 enter{" "}
        <code>=INDEX(Inputs!$G2:$K2,1,A$1)</code>, copy across and down. INDEX selects the source
        column named in the heading. This five-column example is an optional extension; the
        movable-values method above works with your chosen key length.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        All images are actual Excel worksheet-range captures; application side panes are excluded.
        Microsoft references:{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-gb/excel/move-or-copy-cells-rows-and-columns">
          moving columns
        </a>
        ,{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/overview-of-formulas-in-excel-ecfdc708-9162-49e8-b993-c311f47ca173">
          formulas and references
        </a>
        , and{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/wraprows-function-796825f3-975a-4cee-9c84-1bbddf60ade0">
          WRAPROWS
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#transposition`}>
          Open the transposition tool ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/decks/cipher-analysis/`}>
          Explore cipher patterns →
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
